---
scope: src/tools/init/writeMethodology
intent: >
  writeMethodology is the bridge between the canonical AIDE docs under
  docs/ and the host project's agent config file (CLAUDE.md for Claude
  Code, .cursorrules for Cursor, whatever the detected framework uses).
  It reads the canonical docs at runtime, composes them into a
  methodology block wrapped with the framework's expected shape, and
  writes the block into the host's config file exactly once. Success
  is a host project whose installed methodology is a byte-faithful
  render of the canonical source with only a framework-specific
  wrapper around it.
outcomes:
  desired:
    - The installed methodology block is a faithful render of the
      canonical docs read from `docs/` at runtime. A diff between the
      installed block and the concatenated source docs shows only the
      framework-specific wrapper (header, marker comments), never
      content changes, paraphrases, reorderings, or summaries.
    - The helper is idempotent through a single marker comment that
      identifies an already-installed block. A re-run on a host
      project that already has the block changes nothing on disk and
      reports the status as `exists`.
    - Content outside the marker-bounded block is preserved verbatim
      across installs. A user's existing config content, edits above
      or below the block, and any other agent-facing instructions
      survive the write untouched.
  undesired:
    - Any AIDE doctrine living as a template literal inside this
      helper's source. The canonical docs are the single source of
      truth; duplicating them here creates two sources that drift on
      the first edit to either.
    - The block appended to the config file on every run, producing
      stacked duplicates. The marker check is the only correct shape
      for idempotency here.
    - Rewriting the config file from scratch and losing content the
      user had outside the block. The helper is a surgical insert,
      never a replace.
    - A silent fallback to hardcoded content when the canonical docs
      are missing or unreadable. If `docs/` cannot be read, the helper
      must fail loudly, because shipping stale literals is exactly
      the failure mode this architecture exists to prevent.
---

## Context

The host project's config file is the primary documentation surface
the agent reads on every session. Whatever lives in that file *is*
what the agent believes about the project. If the methodology block
there drifts away from the canonical docs, the agent is trained on
stale doctrine and no human is notified. This helper is the single
point at which canonical content crosses from the repo into a host
project, so the burden of making the crossing faithful lives here and
nowhere else.

The framing the parent spec locks in — "this submodule is a renderer,
not a source" — shows up in this helper as a hard rule: reading from
`docs/` at runtime is the only acceptable way to get methodology text
into the block that lands on the host's disk.

## Strategy

**Read from `docs/` at runtime, compose at call time, never embed.**
The canonical docs are plain Markdown files the maintainer edits in
place. This helper reads them on every call, concatenates them in a
stable order, wraps them with the framework-specific header and
marker comment, and writes the result. No caching across process
boundaries, no build-time generation of the literal, no "snapshot"
of the docs checked into this helper's source. The cost of reading a
handful of short files at init time is invisible; the cost of letting
a stale literal ship is not.

**Idempotency via a single marker comment.** The block is wrapped
with a marker both before and after the content. On re-run the
helper looks for the opening marker; if present, it reports `exists`
and returns. It does not compare content against the current docs
and it does not upgrade stale blocks in place — that is the job of a
future explicit `aide_update` path, not this helper. Silent upgrades
would hide exactly the kind of drift a maintainer needs to see.

**Fail loudly when the canonical source is unreachable.** If the
docs cannot be read, the helper returns an error rather than falling
back to an inline default. There is no valid "graceful degradation"
here, because every failure mode of this helper that ships stale or
invented content is worse than a failed init. An init that aborts
with "docs/aide-spec.md not found" is a bug report; an init that
silently ships old doctrine is an invisible regression.

**Outside-the-block content is sacred.** The file can contain
anything else — a project's own CLAUDE.md notes, unrelated agent
instructions, a team's private conventions. This helper only owns
the region between its markers; everything else is passed through
byte-for-byte on every operation.

## Good examples

A maintainer edits `docs/aide-spec.md` to clarify the cascading
intent rule. They release a new version of the MCP server. A host
project runs `aide_init` again; the methodology block in its
CLAUDE.md is unchanged (because the marker already exists) but the
maintainer knows the fix is available behind a future explicit
update flow. The rule "re-init does not upgrade in place" held.

A host project runs `aide_init` for the first time. The resulting
block in CLAUDE.md reads as an identifiable region: an opening marker
comment, a header naming the methodology, the full body of each
canonical doc in a stable order with their original section
hierarchies intact, and a closing marker comment. Anything the
developer had in CLAUDE.md before the run is still there, untouched,
above or below the new block.

An init runs on a host project where the maintainer has manually
pasted earlier AIDE rules into CLAUDE.md outside the block. The
helper writes its marker-bounded block in the correct place and
leaves the pasted text alone. The developer sees two adjacent
methodology regions in their file, notices the duplication, deletes
the older hand-pasted copy, and is left with a single source of
truth that will stay current on future edits to `docs/`.

## Bad examples

A block installed into CLAUDE.md that reads "## AIDE — Autonomous
Intel-Driven Engineering" when the canonical docs say "Autonomous
Intent-Driven Engineering". The block was composed from a string
literal in this helper's source that was written from memory. The
single-source-of-truth invariant failed at the helper level; the host
project now teaches its agent a version of AIDE that contradicts the
canonical docs.

A re-run on an already-initialized project that appends a second
block below the first, producing a CLAUDE.md with two copies of the
methodology and two pairs of marker comments. The marker check was
skipped or misaligned; the idempotency invariant is broken; the file
is now corrupted.

An init run on a project whose CLAUDE.md contains the team's own
conventions above the marker block. The helper rewrites the file
from scratch "to ensure a clean state" and wipes out the team's
content. The surgical-insert invariant was violated; the user trusted
the tool and lost work.

An init whose `docs/aide-spec.md` has been accidentally moved. The
helper catches the missing file and falls back to an embedded
default, silently shipping a six-month-old copy of the doctrine. The
developer believes they got the current methodology; they did not;
no one will notice until the drift causes a contradiction in a
downstream decision.
